      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Long-term effects of Kamp K'aana, a 2-week residential weight management camp, on body mass index (BMI) measures were evaluated on 71 of 108 (66%) obese youth 10 to 14 years of age. Measures were obtained at 11-month study follow-up (n\u200a=\u200a38) or extracted from medical record (n\u200a=\u200a33). Compared with baseline, BMI increased (P\u200a<\u200a0.001), but both BMI percentile and BMI z score decreased (98.7\u200a±\u200a1.0 to 97.3\u200a±\u200a6.7 and 2.34\u200a±\u200a0.30 to 2.23\u200a±\u200a0.34, P\u200a<\u200a0.001). A decrease in BMI z score of ≥0.2 units was seen in 27% of the participants (P\u200a<\u200a0.001). The short program has sustained effect."},
